# Lecture 10D

## Chromosome Aneuploidy

## Chapter 9

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 10, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish polyploidy, aneuploidy, trisomy, and monosomy.
- Predict gametes after nondisjunction in meiosis I or II.
- Relate trisomy 21 to chromosome segregation.
- Compare prenatal screening with diagnostic chromosome testing.

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Aneuploidy

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chromosome Alterations*

@body

- Large-scale chromosomal alterations in humans and other mammals often lead to spontaneous abortions (miscarriages) or cause a variety of developmental disorders
- Plants tolerate such genetic changes better than animals do

=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


@top-left

- About 15% of all pregnancies terminate in spontaneous abortion.
- many (\~50%) due to chromosomal abnormalities

@bottom-left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_002.png)

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_001.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chromosome Errors*

@body

- [*Euploid*]{underline=true} &#8211; organism where chromosome number is an exact multiple of the haploid number
  - [*balanced translocation*]{underline=true} &#8211; non-homologous chromosomes interchange parts, but all parts are present
- [*Aneuploid*]{underline=true} &#8211; organism where chromosome number is NOT an exact multiple of haploid number
  - [*unbalanced translocation*]{underline=true} &#8211; some or part of the genome is missing or duplicated.
- [*Polyploid*]{underline=true} &#8211; organisms containing more than two paired (homologous) sets of chromosomes.

=== layout: section

# Polyploid

=== layout: two-panels

# *Polyploid*

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_003.png)

@right

- Polyploid cells and organisms are those containing more than two paired sets of chromosomes.
  - Polyploidy is found in some organisms and is especially common in plants.
- Most Eukaryotes are diploid
  - one set inherited from each parent.
- [Estimated that 30&#8211;80% of living plant species are polyploid.]{color=red}
  - [Human breeding and cultivation methods have tended to produce these variants.]{color=red}
- Polyploid plants are usually unable to interbreed with their diploid ancestors

=== layout: one-panel

# *Polyploidy*

@body

- Polyploidy is a condition in which an organism has more than two complete sets of chromosomes
  - Triploidy (3n) is three sets of chromosomes
  - Tetraploidy (4n) is four sets of chromosomes
- [Polyploidy is common in plants, but not in animals]{color=red}
- Polyploids are more normal in appearance than aneuploids

=== layout: section

# Aneuploid

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Aneuploid*

@left

- Aneuploid &#8211; organism where chromosome number is [NOT an exact multiple of haploid number]{color=red}
  - [*Trisomy*]{underline=true} &#8211; diploid with extra copy of an individual chromosome
  - [*Monosomy*]{underline=true} &#8211; diploid with missing copy of an individual chromosome

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_004.png)

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_005.png)

- [trisomy, 21]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# *Most Common Trisomy*

@body

- Trisomy 13 (Patau Syndrome)
  - more than 80% don't survive more than a few weeks, very rarely live into their teens.
  - low birthweight, brain-structure problems, and undeveloped facial features
- Trisomy 18 (Edward's Syndrome)
  - many babies with the condition don't survive to birth; of those who survive, less than 10% live to reach age 1.
  - born very small and frail, have many serious health problems and physical defects
- Trisomy 21 (Down Syndrome)
  - mild to moderate issues with thinking, reasoning, and understanding; tend to have certain physical features in common

=== layout: two-panels

